Qt 6.11's src/multimedia/CMakeLists.txt unconditionally compiles the SSE2,
SSSE3, and AVX2 video-frame conversion helpers on macOS. On Apple Silicon
(single-arch arm64) clang rejects the resulting x86 SIMD flags with:

    clang++: error: unsupported option '-msse2' for target 'arm64-apple-darwin'
    clang++: error: unsupported argument 'haswell' to option '-march='

The upstream `EXCLUDE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES arm64` argument on the AVX2 block does
NOT help here. Reading QtSimdHelpers.cmake:

    if(simd_flags_expanded AND osx_architectures AND arg_EXCLUDE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES)
        list(REMOVE_ITEM osx_architectures ${arg_EXCLUDE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES})
        list(TRANSFORM osx_architectures REPLACE "^(.+)$" "-Xarch_\\1;${simd_flags_expanded}" ...)
    endif()

...it only strips the SIMD flag when OSX_ARCHITECTURES is a multi-arch list
(universal builds). On a single-arch arm64 build, `osx_architectures` is empty
or a single entry, the branch is bypassed, and the raw -msse2 / -march=haswell
flags reach clang.

Wrap all three x86-specific SIMD parts (sse2, ssse3, arch_haswell) in a
condition that skips them when targeting Apple + arm64. On Intel macs, Linux,
and Windows the calls run as before. The generic (non-SIMD)
qvideoframeconversionhelper.cpp handles the arm64 case.

Verified against qtmultimedia v6.11.1.

--- a/src/multimedia/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/src/multimedia/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -160,6 +160,7 @@
     CONDITION (LINUX OR ANDROID) AND QT_FEATURE_library
     SOURCES qsymbolsresolveutils.cpp qsymbolsresolveutils_p.h)

+if(NOT (APPLE AND CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR STREQUAL "arm64"))
 qt_internal_add_simd_part(Multimedia SIMD sse2
     SOURCES
         video/qvideoframeconversionhelper_sse2.cpp
@@ -176,6 +177,7 @@
     EXCLUDE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES
         arm64
 )
+endif()

 qt_internal_add_docs(Multimedia
